# Matching backward-rule calls
#
# One fact uses the property :b, and a backward rule makes it available
# under the name :B as well. Four forward rules then ask about :B in four
# shapes: with both ends open, with both ends given, and with one end open.
# Each call reaches the backward rule, and each prints a passing test, which
# shows how a backward rule answers calls with and without variables.

@base <https://eyereasoner.github.io/eye/reasoning/bcrule/example1.n3> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/#> .

:a :b :c .

# Backward rule: s :B o holds whenever s :b o does.
{
    ?s :B ?o
} <= {
    ?s :b ?o
} .

# Test 1.1: both subject and object are variables.
{
    ?s :B ?o
} => {
    :TEST :PASS 1.1
} .

# Test 1.2: both subject and object are given.
{
    :a :B :c
} => {
    :TEST :PASS 1.2
} .

# Test 1.3: the subject is given, the object is a variable.
{
    :a :B ?o
} => {
    :TEST :PASS 1.3
} .

# Test 1.4: the subject is a variable, the object is given.
{
    ?s :B :c
} => {
    :TEST :PASS 1.4
} .
